as previously announced students were  set to return to the classroom on  january 10th  following an extended winter break  today we are announcing a one-week  remote learning period beyond january  10th to help ensure schools  can implement enhanced measures  for the safe return of in-person  learning  we know that our youth learn best in a  classroom setting it is our goal to  ensure they can return to the classroom  as quickly as possible  with that i'll pass it over to minister  cullen to provide further details on the  announcement today  on december 22nd our government made the  announcement to extend winter break  until january 10th  today we are announcing a phased-in  approach  for return to school in january  and are shifting to a one-week remote  learning period  for most students after the holiday  break  this phased-in approach  will allow school divisions and schools  to have more time to prepare operational  plans  including planning for potential  staffing shortages  and expected absenteeism due to  isolation requirements  discussions with manitoba education's  response planning team have reinforced  the importance of this extra time to  plan for a return to class as soon as  possible  this planning team has been in place  since march of 2020 and it includes the  manitoba association of school  superintendents  the manitoba school board association  the manitoba association of school board  officials  the manitoba teachers society  manitoba federation of independent  schools  and public health officials  this will ensure public health officials  have more time to assess the effects of  the omicron byron  effective january 10th this phased-in  approach will allow students of critical  service workers in kindergarten to grade  six  and at-risk students and students with  special learning needs in kindergarten  to grade 12 to attend school  before and after child care programs  will remain open for students of  critical service workers and students  with special needs who are attending  school this week  all other students will learn remotely  until they return on january 17th  this extra week of remote learning will  provide time for distribution of  additional masks and rapid tests to  schools across the province  access to rapid tests for all manitobans  will be expanded  as the rapid testing kit supply  increases here in manitoba  particularly for schools with high case  counts of the covet 19.  we will also be distributing more than 5  million additional child and adult  medical masks over the next eight weeks  to increase existing supply in schools  this time will also provide teachers and  those over 18 who are eligible to get  their booster shots and students five  and up  to get their second dose  to improve accessibility and increase  immunization rates after our  school-based clinics will continue  for students  for staff and community members  our government recognizes there are  financial pressures  related to wage agreements for teachers  as well as the ongoing impact of covet  19.  today we are also announcing an  investment of up to 80 million dollars  in new funding for manitoba schools  for the 21  22 year  today's investment will ensure our  school system is supported particularly  during covet  manitoba has exceptional teachers school  staff and leaders committed to ensuring  students  reach their full potential  our investment will enable schools to  continue providing high quality learning  for all manitoba students  with today's funding announcement  our government has invested an  additional  200 million dollars for school  operations this year alone  this includes  45 million for a two percent property  tax offset grant  paid directly to school divisions  we've also increased the operating  budget for schools  this year to date we have invested 63  million to support the covet response  i will say that manitoba has one of the  highest per-pupil covet funding  allocation in canada to date  